{"name":"Ground Truth","hex":"#757577","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ground truth","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Information acquired  by direct observation rather than by inference."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To gather information by direct observation rather than inference."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ground_truth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ground truth","blocks":[{"text":"Ground truth is information that is known to be real or true, provided by direct observation and measurement as opposed to information provided by inference. The term ground truth appeared in remote sensing literature as early as 1972, when NASA described it as essential \"data about ... materials on the earth's surface\" used to calibrate measurements. It was later adopted by the statistical modeling and machine learning communities.\n It is also commonly used to refer to information acquired by direct observation to inform immediate decisions in domains where remote sensing equipment is employed but is imperfect or inconclusive, often in urgent contexts."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ground_truth","wikidata":"Q370766"},{"title":"Truth","blocks":[{"text":"Truth is commonly understood as conformity to reality or fact. It contrasts with falsity or misrepresentation that fails to align with the world. Truth is typically treated as a property of sentences, propositions, or beliefs that describe things as they are. It is closely related to truthfulness, a virtue associated with honesty, and to truthlikeness, a characteristic of theories that approximate the truth."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truth","wikidata":"Q7949"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Alfred_Tarski_1968_%28as-is%2C_cropped%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Alfred_Tarski_1968_%28as-is%2C_cropped%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Alfred_Tarski_1968_%28as-is%2C_cropped%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Alfred_Tarski_1968_%28as-is%2C_cropped%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Alfred_Tarski_1968_%28as-is%2C_cropped%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Alfred_Tarski_1968_%28as-is%2C_cropped%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Alfred Tarski formulated the semantic theory of truth.[43]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlfred_Tarski_1968_(as-is%2C_cropped)_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/What_is_truth.jpg/500px-What_is_truth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/What_is_truth.jpg/1280px-What_is_truth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/What_is_truth.jpg/500px-What_is_truth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An early skepticism about the nature of truth is expressed in Pontius Pilate's rhetorical question to Jesus, asking \"What is truth?\"[46]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhat_is_truth.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Statue_of_Truth.jpg/500px-Statue_of_Truth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Statue_of_Truth.jpg/1280px-Statue_of_Truth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Statue_of_Truth.jpg/500px-Statue_of_Truth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Walter Seymour Allward's Veritas ('Truth') outside the Supreme Court of Canada, emphasizing truth as a guiding norm of law","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStatue_of_Truth.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/1500-1200_BCE_Rigveda%2C_manuscript_page_sample_i%2C_Mandala_1%2C_Hymn_1_%28Sukta_1%29%2C_Adhyaya_1%2C_lines_1.1.1_to_1.1.9%2C_Sanskrit%2C_Devanagari.jpg/500px-1500-1200_BCE_Rigveda%2C_manuscript_page_sample_i%2C_Mandala_1%2C_Hymn_1_%28Sukta_1%29%2C_Adhyaya_1%2C_lines_1.1.1_to_1.1.9%2C_Sanskrit%2C_Devanagari.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/1500-1200_BCE_Rigveda%2C_manuscript_page_sample_i%2C_Mandala_1%2C_Hymn_1_%28Sukta_1%29%2C_Adhyaya_1%2C_lines_1.1.1_to_1.1.9%2C_Sanskrit%2C_Devanagari.jpg/1280px-1500-1200_BCE_Rigveda%2C_manuscript_page_sample_i%2C_Mandala_1%2C_Hymn_1_%28Sukta_1%29%2C_Adhyaya_1%2C_lines_1.1.1_to_1.1.9%2C_Sanskrit%2C_Devanagari.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/1500-1200_BCE_Rigveda%2C_manuscript_page_sample_i%2C_Mandala_1%2C_Hymn_1_%28Sukta_1%29%2C_Adhyaya_1%2C_lines_1.1.1_to_1.1.9%2C_Sanskrit%2C_Devanagari.jpg/500px-1500-1200_BCE_Rigveda%2C_manuscript_page_sample_i%2C_Mandala_1%2C_Hymn_1_%28Sukta_1%29%2C_Adhyaya_1%2C_lines_1.1.1_to_1.1.9%2C_Sanskrit%2C_Devanagari.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Rigveda, an ancient Indian scripture, explored the relation between cosmic truth and individual truth.[111]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1500-1200_BCE_Rigveda%2C_manuscript_page_sample_i%2C_Mandala_1%2C_Hymn_1_(Sukta_1)%2C_Adhyaya_1%2C_lines_1.1.1_to_1.1.9%2C_Sanskrit%2C_Devanagari.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg/500px-Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campa